    "id": "id_1778058403618_jmzl4",
    "title": "💻 Hacking the EZ2024 Fest",
    "summary": "A one-night cybersecurity challenge where I built ethical hacking demos and security awareness tools for EZ2024 engineering festival.",
    "content": "# 💻 Hacking the EZ2024 Fest\n\n> *“You don’t need expensive tools to hack. Knowledge is the real power.”*\n\n---\n\n# 🚀 The Beginning\n\nIn 2024, our college hosted **EZ2024**, an engineering festival focused on innovation, creativity, and technology.\n\nI wanted to build something different.\n\nNot another normal project.  \nNot another simple demo.\n\nI wanted to create something that would:\n\n- Shock people\n- Educate them\n- Demonstrate real cybersecurity threats\n- Show how easily technology can be manipulated\n- Teach people how to stay secure\n\nSo I decided to build a:\n\n# 🛡️ Cybersecurity Hacking Stall\n\nAnd the craziest part?\n\nI built almost everything in just:\n\n## 🌙 One Night\n\n---\n\n# 🤝 Teamwork Behind the Stall\n\nAlthough I built the hacking tools and scripts, the event became possible because of teamwork.\n\nMy friends:\n\n- Hage Kobing\n- Pori Pegu\n- Jinmoyee Thakuria\n\nhelped me:\n\n- Buy components\n- Arrange the setup\n- Manage the stall\n- Handle the crowd\n\nWithout them, the stall would never have been completed in time.\n\n---\n\n# ⚙️ Building the Hacking Tools\n\n## 💾 1. DIY USB Rubber Ducky\n\nA real USB Rubber Ducky costs around:\n\n**₹6000 – ₹8000**\n\nBut I built a working version using:\n\n- Raspberry Pi Pico\n- Custom scripts\n- HID keyboard emulation\n\nfor only:\n\n**₹700 – ₹900**\n\nThe device demonstrated how a simple USB device could:\n\n- Execute automated scripts\n- Simulate keyboard attacks\n- Open command prompts\n- Run payloads automatically\n\nThe audience was shocked to see how dangerous unknown USB devices can be.\n\n---\n\n# 📡 2. WiFi Jammer (Deauther Attack)\n\nUsing an ESP32 microcontroller, I built a WiFi Deauther system.\n\nThis allowed me to demonstrate:\n\n- WiFi deauthentication attacks\n- Network instability\n- Wireless disruption vulnerabilities\n\nThe demo showed how public WiFi networks can be vulnerable to wireless attacks if not properly secured.\n\nPeople were surprised that such attacks could be created using low-cost hardware.\n\n---\n\n# 🛑 3. Evil Twin WiFi Attack\n\nUsing two ESP8266 boards, I demonstrated an Evil Twin Attack setup.\n\nThe setup worked like this:\n\n- One ESP8266 continuously disconnected users from the original network\n- The second ESP8266 created a fake WiFi network with the same name\n\nThis demonstrated how attackers can trick users into connecting to fake networks.\n\n⚠️ Important:\n\nThis was shown strictly for educational awareness purposes.  \nNo credentials or personal data were collected.\n\nThe goal was to teach people:\n\n> *Always verify WiFi networks before connecting.*\n\n---\n\n# 🔑 4. Raspberry Pi Pico Mobile Unlocking Demo\n\nTo make the stall even more interesting, I built a proof-of-concept password brute-force demonstration using Raspberry Pi Pico.\n\nThe demo was designed to explain:\n\n- Why weak PINs are dangerous\n- How brute-force attacks work\n- The importance of strong authentication\n\nI never used the tool against any real device.\n\nIt was purely an educational awareness demonstration.\n\n---\n\n# 🔥 Crowd Reactions\n\nOur stall quickly became one of the most crowded attractions at EZ2024.\n\nPeople were:\n\n- Curious\n- Excited\n- Shocked\n- Interested in cybersecurity\n\nMany students and visitors had never seen practical cybersecurity demonstrations before.\n\nSome people even offered money to buy the tools.\n\nBut I refused.\n\nBecause in the wrong hands, such tools can be dangerous.\n\nFor me, this project was about:\n\n# 🛡️ Education, Not Exploitation\n\n---\n\n# 📚 What This Experience Taught Me\n\n## 💡 Knowledge Is More Powerful Than Expensive Tools\n\nMost of the hardware I used was extremely affordable.\n\nBut knowledge made the real difference.\n\n---\n\n## 🛡️ Cybersecurity Awareness Matters\n\nMany people underestimate digital threats until they see them in action.\n\nPractical demonstrations create real awareness.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Innovation Can Happen Fast\n\nThe entire setup was built in one night under pressure.\n\nSometimes constraints force creativity.\n\n---\n\n# 🚀 Final Thought\n\nEZ2024 became more than just a college festival for me.\n\nIt became proof that:\n\n> **Cybersecurity is not about fear.**  \n> **It is about understanding technology deeply enough to protect it.**\n\nAnd sometimes…\n\n> *One night of curiosity can create something unforgettable.* 💻✨",
